#{[17592186491202 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491202 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491202 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491202 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the slope and $y$-intercept of the line $y=-2x+3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491202 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd155880-954d-5531-a956-9c38dd5d225e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491202 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation is in slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ where $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFor the equation $y=-2x+3,$ we see that the slope is $m=-2$ and the $y$-intercept is $b=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491207 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491207 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491207 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491207 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(4,1)$ and $(0,9).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491207 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e727ac27-525d-538d-9196-20a878c07523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491207 #edb/ref 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-1}{0-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{-4} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,9),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=9.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=9$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-2x+9.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
